<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ansgar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">801–865</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Saint Ansgar, Apostle of the North</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ansgar was a Frankish monk and missionary, known as the Apostle of the North. Trained at Corbie and Corvey, he preached in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, where he founded the first church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birka">Birka</a>. He became archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> in 831 and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> sack of Hamburg in 845, head of the united see of Hamburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ansgar.jpg" alt="Ansgar" width="320" height="730" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Siegfried Detlev Bendixen / After Hans Bornemann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bendixen_Ansgar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
